  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1125 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nThe latest surface analysis depicts a cold front crossing over\nBirmingham, AL with post frontal stratus blanketing our\nsoutheastern zones. Surface high pressure over the Northern\nPlains will be pushed southeasterly as an upper low and\nassociated trough dig on the western Rockies. As high pressure\nsinks south, skies will clear with a brisk northerly wind,\ntemperatures will fall generally in the 30s for Tuesday morning.\nWinds will remain elevated enough to prohibit perfect radiational\ncooling conditions, so temperatures were nudged upwards slightly\nfrom the NBM.\n\nThe aforementioned trough will continue to dig deep across the\nCONUS and stem apart into a northern upper low and a southern\nstream. The southern stream will be our forecast driver for\nprecipitation on Wednesday. The NBM is aligning better with the\nwarmer solution. PW values appear rather lackluster, at or below\nthe 75th percentile albeit the track and projected slow moving\nnorthward progression should bring beneficial rainfall to areas\neast of Memphis. There are medium chances (30-60%) of receiving\nan inch or more of precipitation through early Thanksgiving\nmorning in the aforementioned area.\n\nThe biggest forecast change appears to be during the phasing of\nthe two systems, which has trended a slower progression. As a\nresult, precipitation chances are likely to linger through much\nof the holiday for northern portions of the region. Once the two\nsystems have phased into one, the low will skirt across central\nKentucky. Some wrap-around showers may mix in a few snowflakes\nacross the Missouri Bootheel and extreme northeast Arkansas, but\nmodel soundings do not favor heavy saturation in the DGZ, which\nwould prohibit flakes from forming. While it cannot be ruled out\ndue to the minimal moisture, there are low probabilities of a\ndusting of snow in our northern zones, but impacts are not\nexpected.\n\nFriday through much of the weekend will be pleasant as high\npressure encompasses much of the southeast. This will result in\nreturn flow emerging and filtering in warmer and less dry air\nwith temperatures creeping back into the 60s for the weekend.\n\nDNM",
